<section class="section preamble-section" id="preamble" data-section="preamble">

        <div class="preamble-hero">
          <div class="preamble-hero-overlay">
            <span class="preamble-hero-eyebrow">Reclaim your digital future</span>
            <h2 class="preamble-hero-title">Technology education and the fight for the East Coast</h2>
            <p class="preamble-hero-sub">Proposing a new qualification in Resilient Technologies.</p>
            <span class="preamble-cta-label">Jump to:</span>
            <div class="preamble-hero-cta">
              <a href="#preamble-body" class="preamble-btn">The why</a>
              <a href="#overview" class="preamble-btn">The qualification</a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

  <div class="preamble-reflect"></div>

  <div class="preamble-body" id="preamble-body">

    <div class="preamble-columns">

      <div class="preamble-col">
        <p class="section-lead">There&rsquo;s a common assumption that technological progress leads to increased productivity and societal advancement. For places like the East Coast of Aotearoa New&nbsp;Zealand, the reality is more complicated.</p>
        <h3 class="section-subhead">Precarity breeds innovation</h3>
        <p>We can&rsquo;t take connectivity and power for granted, and we can&rsquo;t assume corporate goodwill. Climate change, isolation, government restraint, and economic imbalance combine to <strong class="highlight">require resilience and self-sufficiency in our collective infrastructure and skill-base</strong>.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="preamble-col">
        <h3 class="section-subhead">Digital rents keep increasing</h3>
        <p>Access to market-leading online software is heavily subsidised by investor capital. And when that bargain ends, <strong class="highlight">we get enshittification</strong>: the services get worse, the rent goes up, and inequalities are exacerbated by the inability for most of us to access these powerful tools.</p>
        <p><strong class="highlight">Students in regions like Tair&amacr;whiti are starting behind before they&rsquo;ve begun</strong>: many don&rsquo;t have access to regular computing, let alone the subscriptions to the bleeding edge that their peers at institutions in large centres can take for granted.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="preamble-col">
        <h3 class="section-subhead">AI interferring with learning</h3>
        <p>The rapid proliferation of incredible AI tools has put formal learning at odds with itself. Students across every desk-based discipline are expected to be fluent in these AI tools to be employable; meanwhile those same tools are threatening the entry-level roles they&rsquo;re training for.</p>
        <p>Sadly, like social media before it, most of our AI use is intercepting the development of the very things employers say they need most: thoughtfulness, reasoning, the ability to sit with a problem, and the tolerance for failure. <strong class="highlight">In learning, friction is a feature not a bug</strong>; when we remove the friction, we remove the learning itself.</p>
      </div>

    </div>{# /preamble-columns #}

    <div class="preamble-remaining">

      <h3 class="section-subhead">Guided by realities</h3>
      <p>To <span class="highlight">complement the burgeoning regional STEMM initiatives in intermediate and secondary education</span>, let&rsquo;s have a tertiary qualification that recognises the realities of life in Tair&amacr;whiti and the societal pressures emerging globally.</p>
      <p>A qualification recognising:</p>

      <ol class="principles-list">

        <li class="principle">
          <div class="principle-card" role="button" tabindex="0">
            <div class="principle-front">
              <span class="principle-num">01</span>
              <span class="principle-hint">Tap to read &rarr;</span>
              <h4 class="principle-heading">Platform dependency isn&rsquo;t improving productivity</h4>
            </div>
            <div class="principle-back">
              <p>Paying perpetual rents to Microsoft, Google, Anthropic, OpenAI, Amazon, SpaceX et al isn&rsquo;t economically sustainable, especially for under-resourced communities. <strong class="highlight">These companies with trillion-dollar valuations will realise that value through price increases and pushing down on the professional job market</strong>. In regions like Tair&amacr;whiti, the sustainable answer is to be resourceful and own our tools.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li class="principle">
          <div class="principle-card" role="button" tabindex="0">
            <div class="principle-front">
              <span class="principle-num">02</span>
              <span class="principle-hint">Tap to read &rarr;</span>
              <h4 class="principle-heading">400 million devices don&rsquo;t have to be e-waste</h4>
            </div>
            <div class="principle-back">
              <p>Microsoft&rsquo;s decision to prevent certain computers from being upgraded to Windows&nbsp;11 is destining up to 400 million capable machines to the landfill. At the same time, <strong class="highlight">Windows 11 is widely regarded as an enshittified operating system. Modern Linux gives these devices a long working life</strong>. Reclaiming hardware is both a practical skill and a valuable demonstration of ownership and self-reliance.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li class="principle">
          <div class="principle-card" role="button" tabindex="0">
            <div class="principle-front">
              <span class="principle-num">03</span>
              <span class="principle-hint">Tap to read &rarr;</span>
              <h4 class="principle-heading">Infrastructure failure isn&rsquo;t a what-if scenario</h4>
            </div>
            <div class="principle-back">
              <p>Cyclone Gabrielle and every other storm that&rsquo;s followed reminds us we live in a state of fragility. For Tair&amacr;whiti and regions like it, <strong class="highlight">our access to power, the internet, and our supply chains is precarious</strong>. Our future is one where the solar keeps running, shipping lanes are open, the drones are getting where we can&rsquo;t, and the community&rsquo;s technology still works because the people who operate it, built and own it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li class="principle">
          <div class="principle-card" role="button" tabindex="0">
            <div class="principle-front">
              <span class="principle-num">04</span>
              <span class="principle-hint">Tap to read &rarr;</span>
              <h4 class="principle-heading">AI has broken conventional assessment</h4>
            </div>
            <div class="principle-back">
              <p>Any written assignment is now compromised. The only reliable response is sitting alongside a student and asking them to explain their thinking, modify their code, and defend their choices. <strong class="highlight">We can back our students</strong> through: live demonstrations, code reviews, professional conversations, and real deployments.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li class="principle">
          <div class="principle-card" role="button" tabindex="0">
            <div class="principle-front">
              <span class="principle-num">05</span>
              <span class="principle-hint">Tap to read &rarr;</span>
              <h4 class="principle-heading">Resilient technology can be taught like a trade</h4>
            </div>
            <div class="principle-back">
              <p>A graduate here should be able to go out and be useful: fix things, solve problems, increase self-sufficiency. <strong class="highlight">Learning by doing, by failing, and by repetition</strong>. Resilient technologists are in the same family as our problem-solving electricians, plumbers, and carpenters.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li class="principle">
          <div class="principle-card" role="button" tabindex="0">
            <div class="principle-front">
              <span class="principle-num">06</span>
              <span class="principle-hint">Tap to read &rarr;</span>
              <h4 class="principle-heading">There is no qualification like this yet</h4>
            </div>
            <div class="principle-back">
              <p>Imagine the technologist who can host their own AI models, stand up their own servers, build and fly drones, configure solar, and deploy all of it in service of their community. The foundations are developing in regional intermediate and secondary initiatives in STEMM, but <strong class="highlight">that person has no tertiary qualification pathway in Aotearoa yet</strong>.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

      </ol>
      <h3 class="section-subhead">These aren&rsquo;t just luddite worries about technology</h3>
      <p>This is a considered view of what&rsquo;s coming and what a community like <strong class="highlight">Tair&amacr;whiti needs: resilient local people with real technology skills, real tools, real innovation, and genuine ownership of their future...</strong></p>

    </div>{# /preamble-remaining #}

  </div>{# /preamble-body #}

